#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n$f(x) = 2x, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1$\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}$, compute $\\textrm{E}[5X^2 - 2X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\textrm{E}[X] + b\\textrm{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\textrm E [X]$. So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot 2xdx \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X] \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x < 1$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$." :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_{S} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{-2}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}dx + \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2}dx + \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{24}x^{3}\\right|_{-2}^{0} + \\left.\\frac{3}{8}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}(0 + 8) + \\frac{3}{8}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{3}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${1 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E [X^4]$." :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_{S} x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{4}] &= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{4} \\cdot (1 - x)dx + \\int_{1}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} - x^{5})dx + \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{1}{5}x^{5} - \\frac{1}{6}x^{6})\\right|_{0}^{1} + \\left.\\frac{1}{10}x^{5}\\right|_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6} - 0) + \\frac{1}{10}(32 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{30} + \\frac{31}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c823865-e861-5d67-a8dd-12a609a5d5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153869} {:answer/id #uuid "c54e1733-eedb-5175-93f7-00f0ff85140d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153870} {:answer/id #uuid "12aa8baa-a74b-5d99-92f6-b0ee93089a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8339af-aa00-5d70-912d-8abbe719a9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153872} {:answer/id #uuid "60e2dd92-ba17-5b89-b3ee-88e17ff1d026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153873}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e56bd0b8-dd44-5b86-a00c-3b8ef788a24e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153868}] :question/id #uuid "50e246df-b4af-588d-81cb-30d14deb51ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-149703" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{3}{x^{4}},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3x^{3}},\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x < 4$.\n\nCalculate $E[X^{4}]$."}]}
